# The generated puzzle corpus is content-addressed: every chunk's filename and its manifest
# entry carry a SHA-256 of the file's exact bytes. Git's end-of-line conversion rewrites those
# bytes on checkout, so on any machine with `core.autocrlf=true` -- the Windows default -- a
# fresh clone produced a corpus in which *every* chunk failed its hash. Verified by cloning:
# the first chunk hashed 38cea739b0250c17 against a manifest expecting 96fc740c71f810c0.
#
# The failure was total and silent until something checked: `readCorpus` refuses the whole
# corpus as corrupt, and the message blames corruption rather than line endings.
#
# `-text` means git stores and restores these byte for byte, in both directions.
public/puzzles/** -text
